>
> > On 27 Oct 2017, at 15:41, Robert Davies <rwda...@well.ox.ac.uk> wrote:
> >
> > Hi Zan,
> >
> > To impute by taking only sites fixed different in the 30 high body weight birds and 30 low body weight birds should work, and would hopefully get you started.
> >
> > I imagine that you could either
> > 1) try only the 0.5X offspring for this at default settings with K=2 and nGen = 5 (or similar, the program is very robust to nGen) (how many offspring do you have by the way?)
> >
> > Or
> >
> > 2) Make two fake reference haplotypes, with each of the two reference haplotypes corresponding to whether the HBW or LBW birds contained the reference or alternate allele, and then impute using only a single iteration as shown in this acceptance test
> > https://github.com/rwdavies/STITCH/blob/master/STITCH/tests/testthat/test-acceptance.R#L773
> > "STITCH can impute with reference panels with only 1 iteration if the initialize with reference data"
> >
> > I suppose the biggest downside is this limits you to only finding sites that are fixed different between populations, and so you would lose out on polygenic effects for exaample (which I imagine are substantial).
> >
> > For the more ideal set of results, did you ever try running the N=60 30X founders + all 0.5X offspring together in a single run with, say, K=10 for a megabase? The N=60 30X will certainly slow it down alot (as the method is not optimized for high coverage samples at all), but it should provide the best possible set of results.
> >
> > Alternatively, phasing the founders using shapeit / duohmm or similar, and then using that as input to impute all samples using STITCH, with either
> > i) N=1 iterations and as described in the acceptance test above, or
> > ii) using the normal N=40 iterations, where the input reference panels are used in the first iteration, and for iterations 2-40 STITCH uses information only from the samples themselves
> >
> > Finally if you have enough offspring (several hundred?) I would hope that imputing using STITCH in the normal way with only the offspring would give sufficiently decent results (and show that sequencing founders is optional in future studies)
> >
> > Best,
> > Robbie
> >
> >
> > On Fri, Oct 27, 2017 at 7:32 AM, Zan Yanjun <yanj...@gmail.com> wrote:
